---
import DocsLayout from '@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { Callout, DocsTable } from '@/molecules/DocComponents/DocComponents';

const MANAGE_ROWS = [
  ['Add', 'Create a tenant for a repository so its team can start working.'],
  ['List', 'See the tenants currently running.'],
  ['Clean', 'Reset a stuck tenant  -  clear stale or failed work back to the start so the team can take another pass.'],
  ['Remove', 'Take down a tenant and its fleet entirely.'],
];
---


<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Concepts', href: '/docs/concepts' }, { label: 'Tenants' }]}>
  <h1>Tenants</h1>
  <p>
    OpenFlows Manager serves multiple organizations and tenants. A <strong>tenant</strong> is one
    repository automation environment within an organization.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you only have one team, you still have "a tenant"  -  you just have one. Think of a tenant as "one
    project and one repository."
  </p>

  <h2>What a tenant is</h2>
  <p>A tenant binds these records and resources:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      An <strong>OpenFlows organization</strong> and its creator-owned Coder workspace identity.
    </li>
    <li>
      A <strong>repository binding</strong>  -  the GitHub repository that team works on.
    </li>
    <li>
      An <strong>orchestrator workspace</strong> that runs that team's pipeline.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How isolation is enforced</h2>
  <p>Tenants are isolated in two independent ways at the same time:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Runtime identity.</strong> Manager derives tenant and repository scope from each authenticated workspace.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Coordination state.</strong> Each tenant has a Redis ACL credential and immutable namespace.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The Manager and Redis checks reinforce one another. Operators must still protect the shared services,
    secrets, and network paths.
  </p>

  <h2>What this means in practice</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Many teams, one server.</strong> Run several independent OpenFlows teams sharing the same
      control plane and coordination store.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No cross-talk.</strong> One team's tickets, workers, and pull requests are invisible to
      another.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Simple operations.</strong> Administrators manage tenants from the control panel  -  add a
      team, list teams, clean up a stuck team, or remove one entirely.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Managing a tenant</h2>
  <p>
    From the control panel, an operator can manage the tenants currently running:
  </p>
  <DocsTable headers={['Action', 'What it does']} rows={MANAGE_ROWS} />
  <p>
    <strong>Cleanup is an important operational tool.</strong> If a tenant's work is wedged and automated
    recovery has already given up, resetting it back to a clean state is the way to get the loop moving
    again.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
